<ProjectLayout title="Orpheus" projectName="Orpheus" backHref="/selected-works-plain#project2">
    <ul slot="metadata">
        <li>Brainwave Sensing AI Chatbot</li>
        <li>Mar. 2024</li>
        <li style="color: #949494; margin-bottom: -1vw;">My Roles:</li>
        <li><dd> Product Design / BCI Dev <br> AI & ML Dev / UIUX Design </dd></li>
        <li style="color: #949494; margin-bottom: -1vw;">Project Type:</li>
        <li><dd> Individual </dd></li>
        <li style="color: #949494; margin-bottom: -1vw;">Core Tech Stacks:</li>
        <li><dd> EEG Hardware / Data Processing <br> Generative AI / Full-stack Dev </dd></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="project">
        <img src="/assets/proj2.png" alt="Project 2">
        <h2>Can a machine listen to the whispers of your soul?</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="project">
        <hr class="custom-line">
        <h3>Initiative</h3>
        <p>In an era of rapid AI evolution, people often ask: where is the line between AI and living beings? A common answer is that AI lacks feelings. But is that really true? What does it even mean to "feel"? And if AI could feel, what value might that bring to us?<br><br>Today, neurotechnology has advanced to the point where mental states can be partially monitored as data streams. This experimental project combines these cutting-edge technologies to create an <b>AI bot</b> with an innovative interactive interface that integrates <b>brainwave signal monitoring</b>. Designed to <b>support well-being</b>, the bot also explores how AI perceives and responds to emotions, including calmness versus tension and cognitive load variations.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="project">
        <h3>Research & Ideation</h3>
        <p>This project was inspired by my first neuroscience course, where I was fascinated by the idea of representing human minds as data. It got me thinking about the potential for a new generation of interfaces (based on <b>BCI</b>) and whether AI could be trained using this data. As a complete newcomer to the neurotech field, I had to delve deeply into academic research and acquire the necessary knowledge for this project, such as how to monitor and process EEG signals. Based on my understanding of the technology's capabilities and potential outcomes, I developed a simple user persona.</p>
        <img class="illustration" src="/assets/i2-2.png" alt="User Persona Images">
        <p>Finding the right hardware was also crucial. I chose a consumer-grade device to make testing accessible to a broader audience, ultimately selecting the <b>Muse 2</b> for its portability and affordability.</p>
        <img class="illustration" src="/assets/i2-1.png" alt="Hardware Images">
    </div>

    <div class="project">
        <h3>Product Design & Development</h3>
        <p>
            My first step was to build a simple prototype to retrieve data from the device and display it in a chart. However, I found that the headset often introduced <b>significant noise</b> when not worn properly or disrupted by user movements. Since these issues are difficult to control, I implemented a data processing algorithm to filter out outliers and normalize the data for better comparison. Artifact removal uses <b>Independent Component Analysis (ICA)</b> to isolate noise from eye blinks and muscle activity. A <b>Savitzky–Golay filter</b> smooths EEG waveforms to improve visualization accuracy.
        </p>
        <img class="illustration" style="margin-bottom: 6vh;" src="/assets/i2-3.png" alt="Prototype Images">
        <p>
            The frontend design was inspired by <b>Neuralink</b>'s functional minimalism, <b><i>EVA</i></b>'s dynamic AT Field visuals, and the sleek mystery of the device in <b><i>Her</i></b>. These elements guided me to create a blackhole-shaped display, using color and particle movements to represent different mental states.
        </p>
        <img class="illustration" style="margin-bottom: 6vh;" src="/assets/i2-4.png" alt="Inspiration Images">
        <p>
            Then, as planned, I integrated the LLM with processed numerical data and corresponding states. This allowed it to recognize basic emotions or at least a binary mental state. I refined the data processing algorithms through several iterations for better accuracy. Using Three.js, I created a dynamic particle system with <b>BufferGeometry</b> and <b>PointsMaterial</b>, where particle movements are driven by EEG data processed via WebSockets, visually representing mental states in real time. The point cloud's circle size represents signal amplitude, color gradients (red to blue) denote emotional valence, and dynamic shape transitions correspond to frequency shifts detected through <b>Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT)</b>.
        </p>
        <img class="illustration" src="/assets/i2-6.png" alt="Dev Log Images">
    </div>

    <div class="project">
        <h3>Outcome</h3>
        <p>
            • Engaged <b>20+</b> participants to interact with the system, gathering valuable insights on usability and the emotional accuracy of the AI's responses. Testing involved scenarios such as listening to calming versus fast-paced music or watching different types of videos.<br><br>
            • The most frequently mentioned word in the feedback was <b>"special"</b>, highlighting how users found the experience unique and unprecedented. Some even felt it understood their emotions better than they did themselves.<br><br>
            • <b>61%</b> of participants agreed that the AI bot has potential well-being support value, helping users recognize and manage their emotions. After each round of testing, participants' self-reported emotional scores were compared with AI-reported scores, achieving an <b>85%</b> accuracy rate in detecting cognitive load changes.<br><br>
            • Building on this foundational interface, future developments could include a more advanced AI mental counselor that integrates domain-specific expertise and enhanced user interactions. Additionally, creative applications like emotion-driven games and AI-generated art, responding dynamically to emotional shifts, present exciting possibilities for entertainment and self-expression.
        </p>
    </div>
